# Regression proofs

Proofs to run the checker over, each with the verdict it should get.

```bash
scripts/build.sh          # the runner needs the executable
test/regress/run.sh
```

`run.sh` runs `@EXE@` over every `*.proof` here and compares what it prints
against the `.expected` file beside it. `scripts/run-ci.sh` runs it as the
`regress` group.

## The convention

`<name>.proof` is a proof in the Eunoia proof format; `<name>.expected` holds
one word:

| expected | meaning | exit |
| -------- | ------- | ---- |
| `correct` | the assumptions are unsatisfiable | 0 |
| `incorrect` | not accepted as a refutation | 1 |
| `incomplete` | accepted, but outside what the semantics models | 2 |
| `error` | the parser must reject the input; no verdict is printed | 1 |

## Cover all three verdicts, not two

`incomplete` is the one that is easy to leave out and the most worth having. It
is what keeps `correct` from quietly overstating what the soundness theorem
covers, so a checker with no `incomplete` test has no evidence that the
distinction still works.

A freshly generated checker returns `incomplete` for **every** proof it
accepts, because the side conditions in `@CALCULUS@/Proofs/Assumptions.lean`
are stubs that model nothing yet. So an `incomplete` test passing today is
weak evidence; it gets strong exactly when those stubs are replaced and other
proofs start coming back `correct`. Watching a test move from `incomplete` to
`correct` is a good way to see the semantics land.

## Where these came from

Proofs copied out of the specification directory the checker was generated
from — a proof is written in the calculus its signature declares, so it travels
with the signature. Add more here directly.
